# Palo Alto GlobalProtect Vulnerabilities Enable SYSTEM and Root-Level Access

Security researcher Martijn van Ramesdonk disclosed five vulnerabilities in Palo Alto Networks' GlobalProtect, the enterprise VPN and endpoint agent deployed across Windows, macOS, and Linux. Two findings were consolidated into CVE-2026-0251, a group of local privilege-escalation flaws with a CVSS 3.1 base score of 7.8. A low-privileged local attacker who has already landed on a corporate endpoint can use these flaws to obtain SYSTEM privileges on Windows or root on macOS and Linux. Van Ramesdonk reported the issues to Palo Alto in early April 2026; the advisories were published August 12, affecting GlobalProtect App versions 6.0, 6.2, and 6.3.

Beyond privilege escalation, the research includes an alleged credential-recovery technique that could extract Active Directory passwords from an affected device. If validated, this chain would let a local attacker pivot from a single compromised endpoint to domain-wide credential access, which significantly raises the severity for enterprise environments. Palo Alto's advisory acknowledges the privilege-escalation impact but the credential-theft angle warrants close scrutiny.

This matters because GlobalProtect is widely deployed in corporate VPN infrastructure. A local attacker on any managed endpoint could gain full system control without needing additional network access. Organizations running the affected versions should patch immediately, and SOC teams should monitor for anomalous GlobalProtect process behavior or credential dumping activity on endpoints. The researcher's public disclosure after the vendor response window adds urgency, as exploit details are now in the open.
